across south africa's two most populous  provinces it was mob rule  for more than a week it started after  the jailing of former president  jacob zuma the government says the mob  was deliberately directed  towards distribution centers key  highways railways and ports  in haoteng and kwazulu natal you  couldn't do  something like that unless it was well  orchestrated  merida has been monitoring violence and  security in south africa for more than  30 years  how close did they come to effectively  destabilizing the state pretty close i  think  look it's  i mean really it's destabilization when  you get the economic damage we have and  the damage to race relations  basically it's because of the fact that  this province  is the key zuma support base  zuma's close family and friends deny  inciting the looting  across social media there are examples  of people using the imprisonment of the  former president  to justify the violence a slow police  response led to vigilante groups taking  the law into their own hands  here men from an indian majority  township in durban  are shooting at black men who've entered  from a neighbouring township  more than 200 people were killed 20 of  them in this area  including 29 year old percy chitauer his  nephew says his uncle was in a car when  he was shot  so kaiser just came then blocked the  road and  started shooting then the driver and my  uncle  the one who got shot like the driver did  not die  driving in those drill right now look at  this place it burn into the ground  the economy already battered by the  pandemic will contract  three percent according to one u.s  investment bank  president cyril ramaphosa admitted that  the government had been caught off guard  and was slow to respond to the violence  could this happen again  i think it could if the state doesn't  get its intelligence act together if it  doesn't  i mean what's disappointed me  is that some of the key instigators have  not been touched we know who they are  we've got to have to watch carefully  what the strategies there's a lot of  threats that they're going to continue  unless zuma is released government has a  list  of 12 people it suspects of instigating  the violence  only half a dozen have been arrested so  far how close  any of the people on the wanted list are  to jacob zuma is  unknown but how and if they are arrested  could be another test of authority for  the government of cyril ramaphosa  bernard smith al jazeera durbin
 

